; rest.eval — rest as a STATE, and what it costs (docs/health-and-healing.md).
;
; Run:  Dungeon.exe -eval tools\EvalScripts\rest.eval
;
; WHAT THIS SUITE DOES *NOT* MEASURE, and why. Rest's headline is a TIME
; MULTIPLIER — the world runs 60x while you rest — and that is invisible here by
; construction: `step` advances SIM seconds directly, so it is already a
; fast-forward of its own. `rest on; step 300` is five minutes of dungeon time
; whether the multiplier is 60 or 6000. The multiplier is a NORMAL-PLAY concern
; (how long the player sits watching) and the harness quite properly cannot see
; it; what it CAN see is reported by the `rest` command's `world x60`.
;
; So what is measured here is the STATE, which is the half with rules in it:
;
;   1. THE TRADE — you come out healed and POORER. Moving attrition off the
;      health bar and onto supplies is the whole point, and this is the row that
;      shows it happening.
;   2. THE THREE WAYS IT ENDS BY ITSELF: fully recovered, out of food, attacked.
;      A state with no duration has to be able to stop, and each of these exists
;      to stop it quietly costing something.
;   3. A DoT DOES NOT BREAK IT but a BLOW DOES — the `quiet` line. You can rest
;      through a poison and simply pay for it; you cannot sleep through a sword.
;
; Lockstep is ON from the start, which also exercises the thing rest does to the
; AI: it forces lockstep while resting and must hand back what it FOUND, not
; assume it was off. Watch for the `warning: lockstep is OFF` line — its absence
; after a rest is the evidence.

logecho on
newgame

timescale 0
lockstep on
seed 7
arena open 9 9

echo === 1. the multiplier the harness cannot see ===
; Bare `rest` REPORTS; only `rest on`/`rest off` change anything. (It toggled at
; first, and this script used it as a query in four places — each of which
; turned the state back on and made the auto-stop rules read as broken.)
rest
rest on
rest
rest off
rest

echo === 2. the trade: healed, and poorer ===
heal
setsupply all food 90
setsupply all water 90
; Mauled but STANDING — a bleed heavy enough to kill outright would measure the
; death path instead of the rest path (10/s for 6s is 60 damage; Brand has 42).
effect bleed 0 3 5
effect bleed 1 3 5
step 6
echo --- mauled ---
party
supplies
; WHAT A REST TO FULL RECOVERY ACTUALLY COSTS. Not a fixed duration — the state
; ends itself the moment there is nothing left to gain, so the honest number is
; whatever it spent getting there. `step 600` is more than enough room; the
; auto-stop decides when it is over.
;
; NOTE what rest does NOT do: it adds no cost of its own. TIME costs, and rest
; is only how you spend a lot of time quickly. An hour resting and an hour
; walking bill the same food and water; `step` bills it either way, which is why
; the auto-stop matters in PLAY (it stops the clock) and not in this table.
rest on
step 600
echo --- recovered, and what it cost ---
party
supplies
rest

echo === 3. an hour of world time, for comparison ===
; The control for section 2: the SAME hour, spent awake. If these two supply
; drops match — and they do — then rest genuinely has no cost of its own, which
; is the claim "rest is a time multiplier, not a regen multiplier" makes about
; the SUPPLY side as well as the recovery side.
; (`step` caps at 200000 ticks, so an hour arrives as 3333 seconds.)
heal
setsupply all food 90
setsupply all water 90
step 3600
echo --- an hour awake ---
supplies

echo === 4. an empty stomach refuses ===
; Resting while a meter is empty spends health to pass time you are already
; losing health for — the one configuration where the state is purely harmful.
heal
setsupply 0 water 0
step 1
rest on
step 1
echo --- rest should have broken on the empty meter ---
rest
supplies

echo === 5. a DoT does not break it ===
; Brand must be HURT here, and that is not incidental: `heal` alone would leave
; the party fully recovered, the auto-stop would fire on the first frame, and
; the run would read as "the bleed broke rest" when nothing of the sort
; happened. Two rules that end the same state look identical from outside —
; which is exactly why `rest` reports WHICH one fired.
heal
consume waterskin 0
consume waterskin 0
consume waterskin 0
effect bleed 0 4 4
step 5
effect bleed 0 1 90
rest on
step 5
echo --- still resting: a bleed is not an attack ---
rest
party

echo === 6. a blow does ===
; Being hit while the world runs at 60x is how a rest becomes a wipe with no
; input, so the blow has to drop the state. The monster is spawned adjacent and
; swings within a few seconds.
spawn skel_warrior 14 11
step 15
echo --- rest should have broken on the blow ---
rest
party
monsters
state
